# One definition, worked out once, used many ways.
#
# A function with no annotations is worked out at its first call, and
# what that call teaches is split in two: the part the body actually
# depended on, and the part it did not. `echo` hands its argument
# straight back without ever asking what it is, so nothing about it is
# Int -- and its type says so with a variable, which is what lets the
# same definition serve the calls below.
#
# `announce` is the other half of the same rule. `string` asks what it was
# given, so the parameter is pinned to what the first call passed and
# the definition means `(Int) -> String` from then on. That is a real
# limit and not an oversight: `string` accepts Int, String and Bool, and
# Pyking has no way yet to say "any of those" in a signature. See
# fail_typecheck_infer_pinned.pyk for the refusal that follows.

def echo(x):
    x

def announce(value):
    'got ' ++ string(value)

number = echo(1)
word = echo('two')
flag = echo(true)

print(number, word, flag)
print(announce(1), announce(2))

# Calling it again with a type already seen costs nothing: the
# definition was worked out once and is not worked out again.
again = echo(3)
print(again + number)

# The functions' own types, printed. `echo` is one signature covering
# every call it will ever see; `announce` is the one shape its body
# allowed.
same = echo
told = announce
